#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# thickness

#% text
# `thickness(t)` is a graphics directive: lines that follow are t times the width of the plot area wide.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# A relative thickness keeps its proportion when the graphic is drawn larger or smaller; `absolute_thickness` gives a width in points.

#% item
# It applies to `line`, `arrow`, `bezier_curve` and `circle`; the edges of faces take theirs from `edge_form`.

#% item
# The default line width is 1 point.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# Lines of growing thickness:

#% cell
graphics(table([thickness(k/100), line([[0, k], [4, k]])], k, range(1, 4)))
#% output
#= image("thickness.files/thickness-1.svgz")

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A thickness cannot be negative:

#% cell
thickness(-0.01)
#% output
#! failure(argument, thickness, 1)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `absolute_thickness`, `dashing`, `line`, `edge_form`
